+        setting), max_concurrent_batches > 1 means execution for this
+        result was typically already dispatched (and often already
+        complete) one or more steps ago, so by the time `.result()` is
+        awaited here the call mostly measures queue-drain wait, not this
+        step's compute time. Confirmed live: a 4-token step reporting
+        ~0.05ms (an implied ~80,000 tok/s against a benchmarked ~114
+        tok/s). Instead, this uses the real wall-clock gap between
+        consecutive calls to this method (which fire once per completed,
+        non-dummy, non-empty engine step, independent of that broken
+        timer), averaged over a short rolling window rather than a single
+        line-to-line diff — draining a multi-slot batch queue can burst
+        several ready results followed by a gap, which would otherwise
+        make individual diffs noisier than true per-step cadence.
+        """
